    "INDEX-1<SEP>Recommend some helpful activities or conversation starters to use at meetups to break the ice.<SEP>- Ask everyone to share their favorite ice cream flavor.\n- Share an embarrassing moment from your childhood.\n- Share an interesting fact about yourself that others might not know.": -6.09375,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>Recommend some helpful activities or conversation starters to use at meetups to break the ice.<SEP>1. Start with a round of introductions - have each person share their name and something interesting about themselves to get the conversation started.\n2. Play a fun icebreaker game like two truths and a lie or would you rather to get to know each other in a fun way.\n3. Have a group activity or challenge that requires collaboration and teamwork to complete.\n4. Share a personal story or experience related to the topic of the meetup to encourage others to open up and share their own experiences.\n5. Use open-ended questions to encourage discussion and build deeper connections with others at the meetup.": -2.890625,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>Recommend some helpful activities or conversation starters to use at meetups to break the ice.<SEP>1. Play a game of two truths and a lie. Each person takes turns sharing three statements about themselves, two of which are true and one of which is false. The group has to guess which statement is the lie.\n2. Organize an icebreaker game such as \"Two Truths and a Lie\" or \"Would You Rather?\" to help people get to know each other in a fun way.\n3. Organize a group activity such as a scavenger hunt or a team-building exercise to help people interact and bond.\n4. Start a conversation by asking open-ended questions about people's interests, hobbies, or experiences. This can help people feel more comfortable and open up about themselves.\n5. Host a social event or meetup with a specific theme or activity, such as a potluck dinner, a game night, or a talent show. This can help create a fun and engaging atmosphere that encourages people to interact and get to know each other better.": -2.625,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>Recommend some helpful activities or conversation starters to use at meetups to break the ice.<SEP>It's great that you're looking to make your meetups more engaging! Here are a few ideas: 1) Icebreaker games like \"Two Truths and a Lie\" or \"Would You Rather\" can help people connect on a more personal level. 2) Group activities like a puzzle or a collaborative drawing can be fun and interactive. 3) Conversation starters like \"If you could travel anywhere, where would you go?\" can spark interesting discussions. Remember, the goal is to make everyone feel included and comfortable.": -3.640625,
